**Default to Server Components, and use a Client Component only for the leaf that needs interactivity or browser APIs: state, effects, event handlers, refs, `window`/`localStorage`, or client-only libraries. Push the `"use client"` boundary as far down the tree as possible.**

**Server Component when you need to:**
- Fetch data close to the source (database, internal APIs) with secrets.
- Keep heavy dependencies (markdown, syntax highlighting, date libraries) out of the browser bundle.
- Render mostly static or data-driven UI.

**Client Component when you need:**
- `useState`, `useReducer`, `useEffect`, event handlers like `onClick`, `onChange`.
- Browser-only APIs, or libraries that use them (maps, charts, rich text editors).
- Custom hooks that depend on the above.

```tsx
// app/product/[id]/page.tsx (Server Component)
export default async function Page({ params }: PageProps<"/product/[id]">) {
  const { id } = await params;              // params is async in Next 15+
  const product = await getProduct(id);     // DB call, never shipped to the client
  return (
    <article>
      <h1>{product.name}</h1>
      <Markdown source={product.description} /> {/* stays on the server */}
      <AddToCartButton productId={id} />        {/* small client island */}
    </article>
  );
}
```

```tsx
// add-to-cart-button.tsx
"use client";
export function AddToCartButton({ productId }: { productId: string }) {
  const [pending, start] = useTransition();
  return <button disabled={pending} onClick={() => start(() => addToCart(productId))}>Add</button>;
}
```

**Remember:** `"use client"` marks a boundary. That file and everything it imports join the client bundle, so a boundary high in the tree ships a lot of JS. Client Components still render to HTML on the server for the first load; they just also hydrate.

**Guardrails:** `import "server-only"` in data-access modules makes the build fail if a client file imports them. Only `NEXT_PUBLIC_` env vars reach the browser.

**Not by importing it, but yes by composition: a Client Component can receive a Server Component as `children` (or any JSX prop) from a Server Component parent, and render it wherever it likes. If you import a component into a `"use client"` file, it becomes client code.**

**Why:** the client module graph cannot include server-only code (database calls, secrets). But JSX passed as a prop is already rendered on the server into the RSC payload; the Client Component just places that output into its tree, like a slot.

```tsx
// collapsible.tsx
"use client";
export function Collapsible({ title, children }: { title: string; children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  return (
    <section>
      <button onClick={() => setOpen((o) => !o)}>{title}</button>
      {open && children}
    </section>
  );
}
```

```tsx
// page.tsx (Server Component)
export default async function Page() {
  return (
    <Collapsible title="Order history">
      <OrderHistory /> {/* async Server Component: queries the DB on the server */}
    </Collapsible>
  );
}
```

`OrderHistory` runs on the server; its HTML/RSC output is passed to `Collapsible`, which only controls whether to show it.

**What can cross the boundary as props:** serialisable values (strings, numbers, plain objects, arrays, Dates, Maps/Sets), Promises (unwrap with `use()` on the client), JSX, and Server Functions (`"use server"`). Not regular functions, class instances or symbols.

**Common uses:**
- Context providers: a client `ThemeProvider` wrapping server-rendered `children` in the root layout.
- Interactive shells (tabs, accordions, modals, carousels) around server-rendered content.

**Gotcha:** the Server Component output is computed once per server render. Toggling client state does not re-run it; to get fresh server output you navigate, call `router.refresh()`, or revalidate after a Server Action.

**A route (or part of it) is dynamic when it needs information that only exists at request time: runtime APIs like `cookies()`, `headers()`, `searchParams`, un-generated dynamic `params`, `connection()`, or uncached data such as a `fetch` or DB query that is not in `use cache`. Everything else can be prerendered.**

**Triggers:**
- `await cookies()`, `await headers()`, `draftMode()`.
- Reading `searchParams` in a page.
- Dynamic `params` not listed in `generateStaticParams`.
- Uncached I/O: `fetch` (not cached by default since Next 15), DB calls.
- `await connection()` to explicitly wait for a request, e.g. before `Math.random()` or `Date.now()`.

**Next 16 with Cache Components (`cacheComponents: true`):** dynamic is no longer all-or-nothing per route. Static parts and `use cache` output go into a prerendered static shell; runtime parts stream inside `<Suspense>` boundaries. This is Partial Prerendering (PPR) as the default model. Segment configs like `export const dynamic`, `revalidate` and `fetchCache` are replaced by `use cache`, `cacheLife` and Suspense.

```tsx
export default function Page() {
  return (
    <>
      <ProductGrid />                 {/* 'use cache': in the static shell */}
      <Suspense fallback={<CartSkeleton />}>
        <Cart />                      {/* reads cookies(): streams per request */}
      </Suspense>
    </>
  );
}

async function Cart() {
  const session = (await cookies()).get("session")?.value;
  return <CartView items={await getCart(session)} />;
}
```

Without the Suspense boundary, Next 16 shows a "blocking route" error in dev telling you to cache the access or wrap it in Suspense.

**Without Cache Components (previous model):** any of those triggers makes the whole route dynamic, rendered on each request; `next build` output marks routes as static or dynamic.

**Tip:** read runtime data as deep in the tree as possible, so more of the page stays in the shell.

**Treat every Server Action as a public API endpoint: inside the action, authenticate the session, authorise the specific operation on the specific resource, validate all inputs with a schema, and return only the data the client needs. Next.js adds CSRF origin checks and unguessable action IDs, but authorisation is your job.**

**Why:** anyone can find the action ID in the client bundle or network tab and POST to it with arbitrary arguments. The page that renders the form being protected does not protect the action.

```ts
"use server";
import { z } from "zod";
import { updateTag } from "next/cache";

const Input = z.object({ postId: z.string().uuid(), title: z.string().min(1).max(200) });

export async function renamePost(_prev: unknown, formData: FormData) {
  const session = await requireSession();                // 1. authenticate
  const parsed = Input.safeParse(Object.fromEntries(formData));
  if (!parsed.success) return { error: "Invalid input" }; // 2. validate

  const post = await db.post.findUnique({ where: { id: parsed.data.postId } });
  if (!post || post.authorId !== session.userId) {        // 3. authorise this resource
    return { error: "Not found" };
  }
  await db.post.update({ where: { id: post.id }, data: { title: parsed.data.title } });
  updateTag(`post:${post.id}`);
  return { ok: true };                                    // 4. minimal return value
}
```

**Built-in protections to mention:**
- Only `POST` invokes actions, and Next compares the `Origin` header with `Host`/`X-Forwarded-Host`, rejecting cross-site calls (configure `serverActions.allowedOrigins` behind proxies).
- Action IDs are encrypted and non-deterministic, and unused actions are removed from the bundle.
- Variables captured by inline actions are encrypted, but do not rely on that for secrets.

**More hardening:**
- Put auth checks in a data access layer so every caller is checked, not just this action.
- Rate-limit sensitive actions (login, email sending) by user or IP.
- Return errors as values without leaking internals; log server-side.
- Set `serverActions.bodySizeLimit` appropriately for uploads.

**Incremental Static Regeneration serves a cached, prerendered page instantly and regenerates it in the background after its revalidation window passes (or on demand), so the next visitor gets fresh content. That is stale-while-revalidate: always respond fast from cache, refresh behind the scenes.**

**Timeline for time-based revalidation (e.g. revalidate after 1 hour):**
1. Build or first request: page is rendered and cached.
2. Within the hour: everyone gets the cached page.
3. First request after the hour: still gets the stale page immediately, and triggers a background regeneration.
4. Once regeneration succeeds, the cache is replaced; if it fails, the old page keeps serving.

**In Next 16 with Cache Components** you express this with `use cache` and `cacheLife`, which has three knobs: `stale` (how long the client may reuse it), `revalidate` (when the server refreshes in the background), and `expire` (after which requests must wait for fresh data).

```ts
import { cacheLife, cacheTag } from "next/cache";

export async function getPost(slug: string) {
  "use cache";
  cacheLife("hours");          // revalidate hourly, expire after a day
  cacheTag(`post:${slug}`);
  return db.post.findUnique({ where: { slug } });
}

// CMS webhook (route handler): refresh on demand with SWR semantics
revalidateTag(`post:${slug}`, "max");
```

In the previous model you would write `export const revalidate = 3600` or `fetch(url, { next: { revalidate: 3600 } })`.

**Unknown paths:** with `generateStaticParams` you prerender popular paths; other paths are rendered on first visit (Cache Components serve the App Shell immediately) and then cached like the rest.

**Trade-offs:**
- Users can briefly see stale data; fine for blogs, catalogues and docs, wrong for balances and inventory counts at checkout.
- Prefer on-demand tags from a webhook over tiny revalidate windows.
- Self-hosting across instances requires a shared cache handler, otherwise each instance has its own cache.

**Keep the page static and isolate the personalised part behind a `<Suspense>` boundary. With Cache Components in Next 16, that is Partial Prerendering: the static shell (including the widget's fallback) is served instantly from the CDN, and only the widget renders per request and streams in.**

```tsx
// app/pricing/page.tsx
import { Suspense } from "react";
import { cookies } from "next/headers";

export default function PricingPage() {
  return (
    <main>
      <Hero />          {/* static */}
      <PlanTable />     {/* 'use cache' + cacheLife('days') */}
      <Suspense fallback={<div className="h-24" aria-busy="true" />}>
        <YourPlanBanner />
      </Suspense>
    </main>
  );
}

async function YourPlanBanner() {
  const session = (await cookies()).get("session")?.value;
  if (!session) return <SignInCta />;
  const plan = await getPlanFor(session); // per user
  return <p>You are on {plan.name}.</p>;
}
```

**Why it works:**
- Only the component that reads `cookies()` becomes dynamic; everything above the boundary is prerendered.
- The fallback is part of the shell, so the page paints immediately and the banner replaces it when ready.

**Options compared:**
- **PPR / Cache Components:** best of both; one request, fast shell, server-rendered personalisation, no client fetch.
- **Static page + client fetch:** the widget is a Client Component calling an API after hydration. Works in any setup, but adds a round trip and a loading flash, and is invisible to crawlers (fine for personal data).
- **Fully dynamic page:** simplest code, slowest for everyone.

**Correctness and UX:**
- Never put user-specific data in a shared cache without the user in the key. `use cache: private` or passing the session id as an argument scopes it correctly.
- Give the fallback the same dimensions as the widget to avoid CLS.
- Keep the personalised query fast; it is now on the critical path for that region only, not the whole page.

**Real-world example:** an e-commerce product page with static details and a "delivery by Tuesday to your postcode" widget.

**The real choice is stateful vs stateless sessions. A database session stores a random id in an HttpOnly cookie and looks up the session server-side on each request, so revocation is instant but every request hits a store. A stateless session puts signed (often encrypted) claims, usually a JWT, in the cookie; verification needs no lookup, but you cannot revoke it before it expires without adding state back.**

**Database (stateful) sessions:**
- Revoke instantly: delete the row (logout everywhere, ban, password change).
- Small cookie, no sensitive claims on the client.
- Cost: a lookup per request (cache it), and a session store to run.

**Stateless (JWT) sessions:**
- No lookup; works well across services and at the edge.
- Revocation needs short expiry plus refresh tokens, or a denylist (which is state again).
- Claims can be stale (role changed but the token still says admin) and cookies have a ~4 KB limit.

**Where to store it:** in a cookie with `HttpOnly` (not readable by JS), `Secure`, `SameSite=Lax` (or `Strict`), a narrow `Path`, and a sensible `Max-Age`. Avoid `localStorage`: any XSS can steal the token.

```ts
import { SignJWT } from "jose";
import { cookies } from "next/headers";

export async function createSession(userId: string) {
  const token = await new SignJWT({ sub: userId })
    .setProtectedHeader({ alg: "HS256" })
    .setIssuedAt()
    .setExpirationTime("7d")
    .sign(secretKey);
  (await cookies()).set("session", token, {
    httpOnly: true, secure: true, sameSite: "lax", path: "/", maxAge: 60 * 60 * 24 * 7,
  });
}
```

**How to choose:**
- Admin panels, banking, anything needing instant revocation: database sessions.
- Small apps or single-user tools: a signed stateless cookie is simple and fine, with short expiry and a server-side secret rotation plan.
- Microservices: short-lived JWT access tokens between services, with a stateful session at the edge.

**Either way:** verify on the server next to the data (`requireSession()` in actions and route handlers), and rotate the session id on login to prevent session fixation.

**Because middleware (renamed `proxy.ts` in Next.js 16) is a coarse request filter, not a security boundary around your data. It runs before routing based on path matchers, so it can miss routes, can be misconfigured, cannot see which resource an action touches, and has been bypassable before. Authorisation must happen next to the data: in Server Components, Server Actions, Route Handlers and a data access layer.**

**Why proxy alone fails:**
- **Matchers drift:** add `/api/export` and forget the matcher, and it is public.
- **Server Actions are POSTs to the page route:** the check has to be inside the action, which can be called directly.
- **No resource context:** it knows the user is logged in, not whether they own invoice 42 (IDOR).
- **History:** CVE-2025-29927 let attackers skip middleware entirely by sending an internal `x-middleware-subrequest` header on vulnerable versions. Apps that relied only on middleware were fully exposed.
- **Performance:** docs explicitly say proxy is not for slow data fetching or full session management.

**What proxy is good for:** optimistic redirects (no session cookie, go to `/login`), headers, rewrites, A/B buckets, locale detection.

```ts
// proxy.ts (Next 16): optimistic check only
export function proxy(req: NextRequest) {
  if (!req.cookies.has("session")) return NextResponse.redirect(new URL("/login", req.url));
}
export const config = { matcher: ["/dashboard/:path*"] };
```

```ts
// lib/dal.ts: the real check, used by every page, action and route handler
import "server-only";
export const getInvoice = cache(async (id: string) => {
  const session = await requireSession();
  const invoice = await db.invoice.findUnique({ where: { id } });
  if (!invoice || invoice.ownerId !== session.userId) notFound();
  return invoice;
});
```

**Rule:** defence in depth. Proxy for UX, a data access layer for security, and checks inside every action and API route.

**Server Actions can only be invoked with `POST`, and Next.js compares the request's `Origin` header with the `Host` (or `X-Forwarded-Host`) header and rejects mismatches. Combined with browsers' default `SameSite=Lax` cookies, a malicious site cannot submit a form to your action with the user's session. You do not add CSRF tokens yourself for actions.**

**The layers:**
- **POST only:** actions never run from a GET, so links, images and prefetches cannot trigger them.
- **Origin check:** modern browsers send `Origin` on cross-site POSTs; if it does not match the host, Next aborts the request.
- **SameSite cookies:** `Lax` cookies are not sent on cross-site POSTs, so even a forged request arrives unauthenticated.
- **Unguessable action IDs:** IDs are encrypted and rotate between builds; not a security boundary, but it raises the bar.

**Behind a reverse proxy or a different public domain,** the Origin and Host may legitimately differ, so allow-list them:

```ts
// next.config.ts
const nextConfig: NextConfig = {
  experimental: {
    serverActions: { allowedOrigins: ["app.example.com", "*.example.com"] },
  },
};
```

**What is not covered automatically:**
- **Route Handlers** (`app/api/*/route.ts`) are plain HTTP endpoints. For cookie-authenticated POST/PUT/DELETE, check `Origin` yourself, use `SameSite` cookies, or require a custom header or token.

```ts
export async function POST(req: Request) {
  const origin = req.headers.get("origin");
  if (origin !== process.env.APP_ORIGIN) return new Response("Forbidden", { status: 403 });
  // ...
}
```

- **GET handlers that mutate** (`/api/delete?id=1`) are CSRF-able because `Lax` cookies are sent on top-level GET navigations. Never mutate on GET.
- **Same-site subdomains:** `SameSite` treats `evil.example.com` as same-site, so a compromised subdomain can still attempt requests; the Origin check helps here.

CSRF protection is not authorisation: actions still need their own auth checks.

**Choose by how fresh the data must be and whether it is per-user. SSG: same for everyone and changes only on deploy, so prerender at build time. ISR: same for everyone but changes over time, so prerender and refresh in the background or on demand. SSR: depends on the request (user, cookies, real-time data), so render per request.**

**Quick guide:**
- **SSG:** marketing pages, docs, blog posts. Fastest, served from CDN, cheapest.
- **ISR:** product catalogues, news, CMS content. CDN speed, data a few minutes or a webhook behind.
- **SSR:** dashboards, search results, carts, anything personalised or truly real-time.
- **CSR:** highly interactive, private, SEO-irrelevant widgets fetched after load.

**In the App Router you do not pick an API; you make caching decisions and Next derives the outcome:**
- No runtime data or uncached I/O: prerendered (SSG).
- `use cache` + `cacheLife("hours")` or `cacheTag` with `revalidateTag`: ISR behaviour.
- Reading `cookies()`, `headers()` or `searchParams`, or uncached data: rendered per request (SSR).

```tsx
// Static shell + ISR-like catalogue + per-request stock
export default function Product({ params }: PageProps<"/p/[id]">) {
  return (
    <>
      <Details params={params} />                 {/* use cache, cacheTag(`product:${id}`) */}
      <Suspense fallback={<StockSkeleton />}>
        <LiveStock params={params} />             {/* uncached: request time */}
      </Suspense>
    </>
  );
}
export async function generateStaticParams() {
  return (await topProducts(100)).map((p) => ({ id: p.id })); // SSG the popular ones
}
```

**Next 16 point:** with Cache Components the choice is per component, not per page (Partial Prerendering), so one page can mix all three.

**Trade-offs:** SSG/ISR can show stale data and build time grows with pages; SSR costs compute and latency on every request.

**Partial Prerendering (PPR) lets one route be both static and dynamic: at build time Next prerenders a static shell (static markup, cached `use cache` output, and Suspense fallbacks), serves it instantly from the CDN, and streams the request-time parts into their Suspense holes in the same response. In Next.js 16 it is the default rendering model when you enable Cache Components.**

**How it works:**
- During prerender, Next renders the tree. Anything that completes (pure code, `use cache` results with a long-enough lifetime) goes into the shell.
- Anything that needs request data (`cookies()`, `headers()`, `searchParams`, uncached fetches, `connection()`) suspends; the nearest `<Suspense>` fallback goes into the shell instead.
- At request time the server sends the shell immediately, then renders only the dynamic holes and streams them in.

**Enabling it in Next 16:**

```ts
// next.config.ts
const nextConfig: NextConfig = { cacheComponents: true };
```

The old `experimental.ppr` flag and `experimental_ppr` segment export were removed; `cacheComponents` replaces them (and `dynamicIO`/`useCache`).

```tsx
export default function Dashboard() {
  return (
    <Layout>                                  {/* static */}
      <Announcements />                       {/* 'use cache': in the shell */}
      <Suspense fallback={<StatsSkeleton />}>
        <UserStats />                         {/* cookies(): streamed */}
      </Suspense>
    </Layout>
  );
}
```

If a component reads runtime data outside any Suspense boundary, Next 16 reports a blocking-route error in dev and asks you to cache it, wrap it, or move the access deeper.

**Benefits:** static-site TTFB and LCP for the shell, with server-rendered personalisation, in one request, without making the whole route dynamic.

**Trade-offs:**
- You must design boundaries deliberately; reading `params` or cookies high in a layout shrinks the shell.
- Fallbacks need correct sizes to avoid CLS.
- Bots get a fully rendered page at request time, so the shell's data must also be available at runtime.

**Hydration is React attaching event handlers and state to server-rendered HTML by rendering the same tree on the client and adopting the existing DOM. A hydration mismatch happens when the client's first render produces different markup from the server's HTML; React then warns and re-renders that part on the client, losing the SSR benefit and sometimes showing wrong content.**

**Common causes:**
- Non-deterministic values in render: `Date.now()`, `new Date().toLocaleString()`, `Math.random()`, generated ids.
- Environment branches: `typeof window !== "undefined" ? A : B`, `localStorage` reads during render.
- Time zone or locale differences between server and browser.
- Invalid HTML nesting: `<div>` inside `<p>`, `<a>` inside `<a>`; the browser fixes the DOM and React finds a different tree.
- Browser extensions injecting attributes or nodes (password managers, translators).

**Fixes:**

```tsx
"use client";
// Render the same thing on both, then update after hydration
function LocalTime({ iso }: { iso: string }) {
  const [text, setText] = useState<string | null>(null);
  useEffect(() => setText(new Date(iso).toLocaleTimeString()), [iso]);
  return <time dateTime={iso}>{text ?? iso.slice(11, 16) + " UTC"}</time>;
}
```

- Use `useId()` for ids, never random values.
- For client-only components, use `dynamic(() => import("./map"), { ssr: false })` from a Client Component.
- Format dates on the server in a fixed time zone and pass strings down.
- For theme flashes, set the class with a tiny blocking script before hydration and add `suppressHydrationWarning` on `<html>` only.

**`suppressHydrationWarning`** works one level deep and is for unavoidable differences (a timestamp, the theme attribute). Do not use it to hide real bugs.

**In Next 16 with Cache Components,** `Date.now()` or `Math.random()` during prerender also errors unless you call `await connection()` or cache the value, which pushes you toward deterministic rendering.

**Use Server Actions for mutations triggered by your own React UI: forms, buttons, optimistic updates; they integrate with transitions, `useActionState` and revalidation. Use Route Handlers (`app/**/route.ts`) when you need a real HTTP API: webhooks, third-party or mobile clients, file downloads, streaming responses, OAuth callbacks, cron endpoints, or GET endpoints that should be cacheable.**

**Server Actions:**
- `"use server"` functions called like normal functions; Next turns them into POST requests.
- Progressive enhancement with `<form action>`, built-in Origin checks, and can call `updateTag`, `revalidatePath`, `redirect` and `refresh` so the UI updates in the same round trip.
- Not for reads: POST only, not cached, and the client queues them one at a time.

**Route Handlers:**
- Standard Web `Request`/`Response`, any HTTP method, control over status, headers and content types.
- Stable URLs that other systems can call.
- With Cache Components, GET handlers can be prerendered or use `use cache`, like pages.

```ts
// app/api/webhooks/stripe/route.ts
export async function POST(req: Request) {
  const sig = req.headers.get("stripe-signature");
  const event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(await req.text(), sig!, process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET!);
  if (event.type === "product.updated") revalidateTag(`product:${event.data.object.id}`, "max");
  return Response.json({ received: true });
}
```

```ts
// app/actions.ts
"use server";
export async function toggleFavourite(id: string) {
  const s = await requireSession();
  await db.favourite.toggle(s.userId, id);
  updateTag(`favs:${s.userId}`);
}
```

**Anti-pattern:** a Server Component doing `fetch("http://localhost:3000/api/products")`. It adds an HTTP hop and breaks at build time; call the data function directly.

**Both are public endpoints:** authenticate and validate inputs in each one. Route Handlers do not get the automatic Origin check, so protect cookie-authenticated mutations yourself.

**`proxy.ts` is what Next.js 16 calls the file formerly known as `middleware.ts`: a single function at the project root that runs before a request is routed, and can redirect, rewrite, set headers and cookies, or respond directly. It is for lightweight request shaping, not data fetching or real authorisation. It now defaults to the Node.js runtime.**

**Good uses:**
- Optimistic auth redirects (no session cookie, send to `/login`).
- Rewrites for A/B tests, feature flags, multi-tenant subdomains, i18n locale detection.
- Security headers, a CSP with a per-request nonce.
- Bot or geography-based routing.

```ts
// proxy.ts
import { NextResponse, type NextRequest } from "next/server";

export function proxy(req: NextRequest) {
  const bucket = req.cookies.get("ab")?.value ?? (Math.random() < 0.5 ? "a" : "b");
  const res = NextResponse.rewrite(new URL(`/home/${bucket}`, req.url));
  res.cookies.set("ab", bucket, { maxAge: 60 * 60 * 24 * 30 });
  return res;
}

export const config = {
  matcher: ["/((?!_next/static|_next/image|favicon.ico|.*\\.(?:png|jpg|svg)$).*)"],
};
```

**Rules and limits:**
- One `proxy.ts` per project (root or `src/`); split logic into imported modules.
- Use `matcher` to skip static assets; otherwise it runs on every request and adds latency.
- `fetch` caching options have no effect inside proxy.
- Next 16 runs it on Node.js by default and the `runtime` option is not allowed in the file.
- Migration: rename the file and the exported function from `middleware` to `proxy` (a codemod exists).

**What not to do:** use it as your only auth check (see CVE-2025-29927, where middleware could be bypassed with a spoofed header), run slow queries, or do redirects that `next.config.ts` `redirects()` can handle statically.

**`"use cache"` marks an async function, component or whole file as cacheable: Next stores its serialised return value keyed by the build, the function's identity and its serialisable arguments (plus captured variables), and reuses it until its `cacheLife` says otherwise or a `cacheTag` is invalidated. It replaces `unstable_cache` and the old `fetch` cache options, and is enabled by `cacheComponents: true`.**

**Levels:**
- Data-level: cache a function like `getProducts()`.
- UI-level: cache an entire component, layout or page.
- File-level: `"use cache"` at the top caches all exports.

```ts
import { cacheLife, cacheTag } from "next/cache";

export async function getProduct(id: string) {
  "use cache";
  cacheLife("hours");             // stale 5m, revalidate 1h, expire 1d
  cacheTag("products", `product:${id}`);
  return db.product.findUnique({ where: { id } });
}

// after a write in a Server Action
updateTag(`product:${id}`);
```

**Rules:**
- No runtime APIs inside a shared cache scope: read `cookies()` outside and pass the value in, so it becomes part of the key.
- Arguments and return values must be serialisable. JSX and children can pass through without becoming part of the key.
- Pair it with `cacheLife`; short-lived caches (`seconds` profile or `expire` under 5 minutes) are excluded from the prerendered shell and become dynamic holes.

**Variants:**
- `"use cache: private"`: may read cookies and headers, cached only in the browser for that user (for prefetching personalised data).
- `"use cache: remote"`: stores entries in a durable shared cache handler instead of per-instance memory; worth it only at a high hit rate.

**Where it lives:** results prerendered at build go into the static shell. Runtime entries default to in-memory per instance (ephemeral on serverless). All caches are scoped to a deployment.

**Trade-offs:** stale data risk, debugging cache keys, and the need to invalidate correctly. Do not cache per-user data in a shared scope without the user id in the key.

**All three invalidate cached data on demand. `revalidatePath` targets a route URL and everything cached for it. `revalidateTag` targets every cache entry tagged with `cacheTag` (or `fetch` `next.tags`) anywhere in the app, with stale-while-revalidate semantics. `updateTag` also targets a tag but expires it immediately, for read-your-own-writes, and only works in Server Actions.**

**Comparison:**
- **`revalidatePath("/blog/[slug]", "page")`:** use when you know the page but not its data dependencies. Coarse: re-renders the route's data; `"layout"` type covers all nested routes.
- **`revalidateTag("posts", "max")`:** precise: the same data used on the homepage, archive and RSS feed all refresh together. The next request may still get stale content while fresh content is generated. Callable from Server Actions and Route Handlers (webhooks).
- **`updateTag("posts")`:** Server Actions only; the next read waits for fresh data, so the user who made the change sees it.

```ts
// data
export async function getPosts() {
  "use cache";
  cacheLife("max");
  cacheTag("posts");
  return db.post.findMany();
}

// user action: show the change right away
"use server";
export async function publish(id: string) {
  await requireSession();
  await db.post.publish(id);
  updateTag("posts");
  updateTag(`post:${id}`);
}

// CMS webhook: background refresh is fine
export async function POST(req: Request) {
  const { slug } = await req.json();
  revalidateTag(`post:${slug}`, "max");
  return new Response(null, { status: 204 });
}
```

**Next 16 note:** `revalidateTag(tag)` with a single argument is deprecated; pass a `cacheLife` profile (`"max"` recommended) or `{ expire: 0 }` for immediate expiry from a Route Handler. There is also `refresh()` in Server Actions to refresh the client router without invalidating any tags.

**Tag design:** collection tags (`posts`) plus entity tags (`post:42`) plus relation tags (`author:7:posts`), so writes invalidate exactly what changed. The docs recommend tags over paths for precision.

**Parallel routes (`@slot` folders) render multiple independent pages in the same layout, each with its own loading, error and navigation state. Intercepting routes (`(.)`, `(..)`, `(...)` folders) render a different route inside the current layout during client navigation. Together they give you URL-addressable modals: clicking a photo opens it as a modal over the feed with a shareable URL, while a refresh or shared link opens the full photo page.**

**Parallel routes:**
- `app/@analytics/page.tsx` and `app/@team/page.tsx` become `analytics` and `team` props on `app/layout.tsx`.
- Slots are not URL segments. Each can stream, error and navigate independently, and you can render them conditionally (e.g. by role).
- `default.tsx` is the fallback when a slot has no match for the current URL after a hard navigation.

**Intercepting routes:**
- `(.)photo` matches the same level, `(..)` one segment up, `(...)` from the root (based on route segments, not folders like slots).
- Interception only happens on soft (client) navigation; a direct visit renders the normal route.

```text
app/
  layout.tsx            // renders {children} and {modal}
  @modal/
    default.tsx         // returns null when no modal is open
    (.)photos/[id]/page.tsx  // modal version
  photos/[id]/page.tsx  // full page version
  page.tsx              // the feed
```

```tsx
// app/layout.tsx
export default function Layout({ children, modal }: LayoutProps<"/">) {
  return <html lang="en"><body>{children}{modal}</body></html>;
}

// app/@modal/(.)photos/[id]/page.tsx
export default async function PhotoModal({ params }: PageProps<"/photos/[id]">) {
  const { id } = await params;
  return <Modal><Photo id={id} /></Modal>; // Modal calls router.back() on close
}
```

**Use cases:** login or share modals, product quick views, image galleries, dashboards with independent panels, split views.

**Trade-offs:** more files and conventions to learn, `default.tsx` pitfalls, and you maintain two renderings of the same content.

**`generateStaticParams` runs at build time and returns the list of dynamic segment values to prerender, for example the slugs of every blog post. Those pages become static HTML. Params not in the list are, by default, rendered on first request and then cached (ISR), or return 404 if you set `dynamicParams = false`.**

```tsx
// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx
export async function generateStaticParams() {
  const posts = await getPublishedPosts();
  return posts.map((p) => ({ slug: p.slug })); // values must be strings
}

export default async function Post({ params }: PageProps<"/blog/[slug]">) {
  const { slug } = await params;
  const post = await getPost(slug); // 'use cache' + cacheTag(`post:${slug}`)
  if (!post) notFound();
  return <Article post={post} />;
}
```

**Key behaviours:**
- Runs before layouts and pages at build; `fetch` and `use cache` calls inside are deduplicated with the page's.
- Nested segments: a child can read the parent's params, or the child can return all combinations (`{ category, product }`).
- With Cache Components, listed params produce a fully concrete static shell; unlisted ones get the reusable App Shell instantly and are upgraded in the background, then cached for the next visitor.
- `export const dynamicParams = false` makes unlisted paths 404 (useful for a fixed set like docs versions).

**Scaling strategy:**
- Prerender only the top N (most visited products, recent posts) and let the long tail render on demand.
- Invalidate with `cacheTag`/`revalidateTag` when content changes, instead of rebuilding.
- Returning an empty array prerenders nothing at build but still caches pages after their first visit.

**Trade-offs:** more prerendered pages means longer builds and more storage; on-demand pages make the first visitor wait for a render. Choose the split from traffic data.

**Validate on the server inside the action with a schema such as zod, return a typed state object (field errors plus the submitted values) instead of throwing, and read it in the form with `useActionState`. Feed the returned values back as `defaultValue`, because React resets uncontrolled fields after a form action.**

**The action:**

```ts
"use server";
import { z } from "zod";

const Signup = z.object({
  email: z.string().email("Enter a valid email"),
  password: z.string().min(12, "Use at least 12 characters"),
});

export type SignupState = {
  errors?: { email?: string[]; password?: string[] };
  values?: { email: string };
  message?: string;
};

export async function signup(_prev: SignupState, formData: FormData): Promise<SignupState> {
  const raw = { email: String(formData.get("email") ?? ""), password: String(formData.get("password") ?? "") };
  const parsed = Signup.safeParse(raw);
  if (!parsed.success) {
    return { errors: parsed.error.flatten().fieldErrors, values: { email: raw.email } }; // never echo the password
  }
  const res = await createAccount(parsed.data);
  if (!res.ok) return { message: "That email is already registered", values: { email: raw.email } };
  redirect("/welcome");
}
```

**The form:**

```tsx
"use client";
import { useActionState } from "react";
import { signup, type SignupState } from "./actions";

export function SignupForm() {
  const [state, action, pending] = useActionState<SignupState, FormData>(signup, {});
  return (
    <form action={action} noValidate>
      <label htmlFor="email">Email</label>
      <input id="email" name="email" type="email" defaultValue={state.values?.email}
        aria-invalid={!!state.errors?.email} aria-describedby="email-error" />
      {state.errors?.email && <p id="email-error" role="alert">{state.errors.email[0]}</p>}

      <label htmlFor="password">Password</label>
      <input id="password" name="password" type="password" aria-describedby="pw-error" />
      {state.errors?.password && <p id="pw-error" role="alert">{state.errors.password[0]}</p>}

      {state.message && <p role="alert">{state.message}</p>}
      <button disabled={pending}>{pending ? "Creating..." : "Create account"}</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

**Why each piece matters:**
- **Server validation is the security boundary.** Client checks (HTML `required`, or the same zod schema in the browser) are UX only, because the POST can be sent without your form.
- **Return, do not throw, expected errors.** Thrown errors go to `error.tsx` and replace the page. Validation failures are normal outcomes.
- **Accessibility:** connect errors with `aria-describedby`, mark fields `aria-invalid`, and use `role="alert"` (or move focus to an error summary) so screen-reader users hear the problem.
- **Pending state:** `useActionState`'s third value, or `useFormStatus` in a child button component.
- **Progressive enhancement:** this works before hydration. Pass a `permalink` as the third `useActionState` argument if the form can render on a different URL than the one that handles it.

**Call `updateTag(tag)` for the tags your mutation affected, inside the Server Action and before any `redirect()`. `updateTag` expires those entries immediately, so the re-render that ships in the same action response, and the next read, wait for fresh data instead of serving stale content. Use `revalidatePath` when a single route is affected, and `refresh()` when the view depends on uncached state.**

**The options in Next.js 16:**

| Call | Freshness for the acting user | Where |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `updateTag("posts")` | immediate: the next read blocks on fresh data | Server Actions only |
| `revalidateTag("posts", "max")` | stale-while-revalidate: old data now, new data later | Server Actions, Route Handlers |
| `revalidatePath("/posts")` | immediate for that path | Server Actions, Route Handlers |
| `refresh()` | refetches the current route payload; caches untouched | Server Actions |

```ts
"use server";
import { updateTag } from "next/cache";
import { redirect } from "next/navigation";

export async function createPost(formData: FormData) {
  const session = await verifySession();
  const post = await db.post.create({ data: { title: String(formData.get("title")), authorId: session.userId } });
  updateTag("posts");               // list pages
  updateTag(`post:${post.id}`);     // detail page
  redirect(`/posts/${post.id}`);    // throws; anything after this never runs
}
```

This works only if the reads were tagged:

```ts
async function getPosts() {
  "use cache";
  cacheLife("hours");
  cacheTag("posts");
  return db.post.findMany({ orderBy: { createdAt: "desc" } });
}
```

**Why not always `revalidateTag`?** With the recommended `"max"` profile it serves the stale entry while refreshing in the background, and the action response does **not** include a re-render. That is ideal for a CMS webhook where readers can lag a few seconds, and wrong when the person who just clicked Save expects to see the change. The single-argument `revalidateTag(tag)` is deprecated in 16.

**Other layers to remember:**
- **Client router cache.** Revalidating inside an action also refreshes the client-side cache for the current route. Other routes the user visited may still be within their `stale` window until next navigation.
- **Multi-instance self-hosting.** Invalidation is per instance unless your cache handler shares tag state (`refreshTags`), otherwise the next request may land on a pod with stale data.
- **Optimistic UI.** For instant feedback before the round trip, combine `useOptimistic` with `updateTag`. The optimistic layer shows immediately and the real data replaces it when the action completes.

**Debugging stale UI:** confirm the read is tagged with exactly the string you invalidate (tags are case-sensitive, max 256 characters). Confirm the invalidation runs before `redirect`. Check whether the data comes from a cache you did not tag, such as an untagged `fetch` or a CDN.

**The classic bug is an insecure direct object reference (IDOR). The action trusts the ID and updates or deletes that row, but anyone can POST to the action with someone else's ID, because every Server Action is a public endpoint. Secure it by deriving the user from the session inside the action, re-reading the row by ID and owner (or checking a permission) from the database, validating the input, and returning only minimal data.**

**Vulnerable vs safe:**

```ts
"use server";

// Vulnerable: no auth, trusts the client-supplied object
export async function archiveInvoiceUnsafe(invoice: { id: string; ownerId: string }) {
  await db.invoice.update({ where: { id: invoice.id }, data: { archived: true } });
}

// Safe
export async function archiveInvoice(invoiceId: unknown) {
  const session = await verifySession();                  // 1. who is calling (throws or redirects)
  const id = z.string().uuid().parse(invoiceId);          // 2. shape
  const invoice = await db.invoice.findFirst({            // 3. ownership / permission
    where: { id, orgId: session.orgId },
    select: { id: true, status: true },
  });
  if (!invoice) return { ok: false as const, error: "Not found" };   // do not reveal existence
  if (invoice.status === "paid" && session.role !== "admin") return { ok: false as const, error: "Forbidden" };
  await db.invoice.update({ where: { id }, data: { archived: true } });
  updateTag(`invoices:${session.orgId}`);
  return { ok: true as const };                           // 4. minimal return value
}
```

**Why the usual defences are not enough:**
- **Hiding the UI** is not access control. The action ID is in the client bundle, and the POST can be replayed with any arguments.
- **zod** checks shape, not ownership. A perfectly valid UUID can belong to another tenant.
- **`proxy.ts` auth** only sees "has a session" and can be skipped by matcher gaps. The Next.js docs note that Server Functions are POSTs to the page route, so excluded paths skip the proxy entirely.
- **Closure encryption** protects values you captured from tampering and reading, but the docs say not to rely on it for secrets, and it does nothing for arguments the client chooses.

**Defence in depth:**
- Put these checks in a **Data Access Layer** (`server-only` module) so every caller (pages, actions, route handlers) goes through the same authorisation.
- **Return DTOs,** never raw rows: return values are serialised to the client.
- **Rate-limit** abusable actions (password reset, invites, AI generation) per user and IP.
- **Re-authenticate** for destructive operations (delete account, change email).
- **Log** denied attempts with user ID and target ID. A spike of not-found results on sequential IDs is an enumeration attempt.

OWASP's IDOR prevention cheat sheet sums up the rule: check authorisation on every object access, and do not rely on unguessable IDs alone.

**Add a `@modal` parallel route slot to the layout that shows the feed, put an intercepting route `(.)photo/[id]` inside that slot that renders the photo in a dialog, and keep a normal `photo/[id]/page.tsx` for the full page. Clicking a photo is a soft navigation, so the URL changes to `/photo/42` and the intercepted route renders in the modal over the feed. A refresh or shared link is a hard navigation, so no interception happens and the full page renders.**

**File layout:**

```text
app/
  layout.tsx                    renders {children} and {modal}
  page.tsx                      feed with <Link href={`/photo/${id}`}>
  photo/[id]/page.tsx           full page (hard navigation)
  @modal/
    default.tsx                 export default () => null
    (.)photo/[id]/page.tsx      <Modal><Photo id={id} /></Modal>
    [...catchAll]/page.tsx      export default () => null   (closes the modal on other links)
```

```tsx
// app/layout.tsx
export default function RootLayout({ children, modal }: { children: React.ReactNode; modal: React.ReactNode }) {
  return <html lang="en"><body>{children}{modal}</body></html>;
}
```

```tsx
// components/modal.tsx
"use client";
import { useRouter } from "next/navigation";
export function Modal({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const router = useRouter();
  return (
    <div role="dialog" aria-modal="true" onClick={() => router.back()}>
      <div onClick={(e) => e.stopPropagation()}>{children}</div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

**Why each piece exists:**
- **`(.)photo`**: intercepts `/photo/[id]` from the same segment level. Matchers count route segments, so the `@modal` slot folder does not add a level.
- **`@modal/default.tsx`** returning `null`: on hard navigation (refresh on `/`), Next.js cannot recover a slot's state and renders `default.tsx`. Without it the route 404s.
- **Catch-all null page:** during soft navigation, a slot keeps its last state when the new URL does not match it. Navigating from the modal to `/settings` would leave the modal open without it.
- **`router.back()`** to close: restores the feed URL and scroll position. Back and forward naturally close and reopen the modal.
- **Shared component:** both `photo/[id]/page.tsx` and the intercepted page render the same `<Photo id>` Server Component, which can be cached with `'use cache'` and `cacheTag`.

**Accessibility:** a real dialog needs focus trapping, Escape to close, focus return to the clicked thumbnail, and a labelled title. Use the native `dialog` element with `showModal()` or a headless dialog primitive rather than a bare div.

**Behaviour summary:**

| Action | URL | Renders |
| --- | --- | --- |
| click thumbnail | /photo/42 | feed (children) + modal (intercepted) |
| refresh / open link | /photo/42 | full photo page + `@modal/default` (null) |
| back | / | feed; modal slot returns to default |

**The capability is the same: code that runs before a request is routed and can redirect, rewrite, set headers or cookies, or respond directly. The Next.js team renamed it because "middleware" made people think of Express-style middleware chains, which encouraged putting too much logic there. "Proxy" says what it is: a network-boundary layer in front of the app, to be used sparingly. The other real change in 16 is the runtime: Proxy always runs on Node.js and cannot be configured to use Edge.**

**What changed concretely:**
- File `middleware.ts` becomes `proxy.ts` (project root or `src/`), and the exported function `middleware` becomes `proxy` (a default export also works).
- Config flags containing "middleware" were renamed, for example `skipMiddlewareUrlNormalize` became `skipProxyUrlNormalize`.
- **Runtime:** Proxy defaults to Node.js, and setting `runtime` in the file throws. The upgrade guide says to keep the deprecated `middleware.ts` if you still need Edge.
- `middleware.ts` still works but is deprecated.

**Migration:**

```bash
npx @next/codemod@canary middleware-to-proxy .
```

```ts
// proxy.ts
import { NextResponse, type NextRequest } from "next/server";

export function proxy(request: NextRequest) {
  if (!request.cookies.has("session")) {
    return NextResponse.redirect(new URL("/login", request.url));
  }
}

export const config = { matcher: ["/dashboard/:path*", "/settings/:path*"] };
```

**What the docs now steer you toward:**
- Static redirects and headers belong in `next.config.ts` (`redirects()`, `headers()`). They run before Proxy and need no code.
- Proxy is for request-dependent logic: optimistic auth redirects, A/B or locale rewrites, multi-tenant host rewrites, adding headers.
- It is **not** for slow data fetching or full session management, and `fetch` cache options have no effect inside it.
- Authorisation still belongs next to the data (Data Access Layer), in pages, Server Actions and Route Handlers. Server Actions are POSTs to page routes, so matcher gaps skip them.

**Good to mention:** there is one proxy file per project, so organise logic into imported modules. Without a `matcher` it runs on every request, including static assets.

**CVE-2025-29927 (March 2025, critical) let an attacker skip Next.js middleware entirely by sending a crafted internal header, `x-middleware-subrequest`, that Next.js used to avoid infinite middleware recursion. Any self-hosted app that enforced authorisation only in middleware exposed its protected pages to anyone. The lesson is that this layer is an optimisation, not a security boundary: check authentication and authorisation next to the data, in every page, Server Action and Route Handler.**

**What happened, in short:**
- Middleware marks internal subrequests with a header so it does not run on itself recursively.
- The server trusted that header on **incoming** requests. Supplying the right value made Next.js believe middleware had already run, so it skipped it.
- Affected: self-hosted apps using `next start` (including standalone output) on versions from 11.1.4 up to the fixes. Patched in 15.2.3, 14.2.25, 13.5.9 and 12.3.5. Vercel-hosted apps were not affected because the platform routes middleware differently. Vercel published a postmortem.

**Why middleware-only auth was fragile even without the CVE:**
- **Matcher drift:** a new route or a refactored regex can fall outside the matcher.
- **Server Actions** are POSTs to the page route, so excluding a path also excludes its actions. Moving an action changes coverage silently (the Next.js 16 proxy docs call this out).
- **Coarse knowledge:** the proxy sees "valid session cookie", not "may this user read invoice 42".

**The pattern the Next.js docs recommend now:**

```ts
// lib/dal.ts
import "server-only";
import { cache } from "react";
import { cookies } from "next/headers";
import { redirect } from "next/navigation";

export const verifySession = cache(async () => {
  const token = (await cookies()).get("session")?.value;
  const session = token ? await decrypt(token) : null;
  if (!session) redirect("/login");
  return { userId: session.userId, role: session.role };
});

export async function getInvoice(id: string) {
  const s = await verifySession();
  return db.invoice.findFirst({ where: { id, orgId: s.orgId }, select: { id: true, total: true } });
}
```

Pages, actions and route handlers call DAL functions. `proxy.ts` stays as an **optimistic** redirect for a fast logged-out experience.

**Defence in depth beyond code:**
- Strip internal or unknown `x-middleware-*` style headers at the CDN or reverse proxy.
- Subscribe to framework security advisories and keep patch versions current. Have a playbook to roll a framework update within hours.
- Add tests that hit protected routes and actions without a session and assert 401, 403 or a redirect, independent of the proxy.

**Use the `instrumentation.ts` file. Its `register()` function initialises OpenTelemetry (the quickest route is `registerOTel` from `@vercel/otel`) so Next.js emits spans for incoming requests, rendering, route handlers and fetches to any OTLP backend. Its `onRequestError` export receives every server error with route context, which you forward to an error tracker. On the client, report Web Vitals with `useReportWebVitals` and catch rendering errors with `error.tsx` boundaries that log the error digest.**

**Server tracing:**

```ts
// instrumentation.ts
import { registerOTel } from "@vercel/otel";

export function register() {
  registerOTel({ serviceName: "storefront-web" });
}
```

Set the standard `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` (and headers) to send traces to Grafana Tempo, Honeycomb, Datadog or an OpenTelemetry Collector. Next.js instruments request handling, the render of each route, Route Handlers and `fetch` calls. Add custom spans around expensive work:

```ts
import { trace } from "@opentelemetry/api";
const tracer = trace.getTracer("storefront");

export async function getRecommendations(userId: string) {
  return tracer.startActiveSpan("recommendations.fetch", async (span) => {
    try {
      span.setAttribute("rec.count_requested", 12); // no PII in attributes
      return await recService.get(userId);
    } finally {
      span.end();
    }
  });
}
```

**Server errors:**

```ts
// instrumentation.ts
import { type Instrumentation } from "next";

export const onRequestError: Instrumentation.onRequestError = async (err, request, context) => {
  const digest = typeof err === "object" && err !== null && "digest" in err ? String(err.digest) : undefined;
  await reportToErrorTracker(err, { digest, path: request.path, route: context.routePath, type: context.routeType }); // await async work
};
```

In production, errors thrown in Server Components reach the browser as a generic message plus a **digest**. Your `error.tsx` shows it ("Reference: 3829...") and your server logs record it, so a support ticket can be matched to the exact stack trace without leaking internals.

**Client side:**
- `error.tsx` and `global-error.tsx` (Client Components) report caught render errors in an effect.
- `useReportWebVitals` (or `instrumentation-client.ts` for setup that must run before hydration) sends LCP, INP, CLS and TTFB from real users to your analytics. Field data reveals the slow devices and networks Lighthouse never sees.

**What makes it useful rather than noise:**
- **Correlate:** propagate trace context to downstream services (OpenTelemetry does this over HTTP headers) and include the trace ID in structured logs.
- **Sample:** keep 100% of error traces and a small percentage of successful ones to control cost.
- **Protect privacy:** no tokens, emails or request bodies in span attributes or logs.
- **Alert on SLOs** (p95 latency per route, error rate) rather than on individual errors.

**Debugging flow:** a user reports a broken page and the digest leads to the server error. The trace for that request shows the slow or failing span (a fetch to the pricing API timing out), which points at the fix.
